val prompts=: ["'Snowballing' is an example of:\nA. probability sampling\nB. non-probability sampling\nC. cluster sampling\nD. using the Christmas vacation constructively\nAnswer:", 'How do girls most frequently learn about masturbation?\nA. through reading about it\nB. being told by peers\nC. self-discovery\nD. observing others doing it.\nAnswer:', "Which of the following is not a 'research purpose'?\nA. triangulation\nB. explanation\nC. description\nD. exploration\nAnswer:", 'The placenta does all of the following except:\nA. supplies oxygen to the fetus\nB. supplies nutrients to the fetus\nC. supplies blood to the fetus\nD. secretes hormones\nAnswer:', "You have been asked to write a response paper to LeVay's research on brain differences between heterosexuals and homosexuals. Which are plausible criticisms of LeVay's work?\nA. Sample size was relatively small.\nB. Gay and straight groups were not equivalent in terms of the number of deaths due to AIDS.\nC. Lesbians were omitted from the study.\nD. all of the above\nAnswer:", "The embourgeoisement thesis suggests that:\nA. capitalists are being replaced by salaried managers with bureaucratic authority\nB. the affluent working class is adopting middle class values and ways of life\nC. white collar occupations are being 'deskilled' by the introduction of new technologies into the workplace\nD. the class structure has fragmented and there are no longer any distinct social classes\nAnswer:", "The 'new racism' refers to:\nA. a subtler form of prejudice, masked by nationalist pride\nB. a post-modern deconstruction of racist ideas to reveal their lack of depth\nC. racist practices found in newly emerging areas of social life, such as cyberspace\nD. an anti-fascist movement which challenges nationalist politics\nAnswer:", "The print revolution of the nineteenth century resulted in:\nA. mass production, mass circulation, and the decline of serious content\nB. the public ownership of newspapers as a shared resource\nC. more people going to the cinema as a new leisure activity\nD. a reduction of 'virtual communities' on the Internet\nAnswer:"]
